  "label": "Context Aware Bacterial Genomic Inference",
  "related": [
    "amino-acid-6d41",
    "influenza-virus-evolution-biology-83d3",
    "fungal-calibrated-8715"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [
    "genomics"
  ],
  "slug": "long-zero-2365",
  "summary": "Three bacterial-genomics works pair method choices with context: core SNP clustering, TF prioritization benchmarking, and the first bacterial cis-eQTL study.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that bacterial genomic inference depends on explicit methodological context rather than a single default. SNP2Cluster-Py frames Core SNP and transmission-cluster analysis as context-aware and pathogen-agnostic, tested on Klebsiella and pneumococcal WGS datasets. BioSensor-X benchmarks evidence integration for E. coli transcription-factor prioritization, with coverage awareness and leakage control. The cis-eQTL study applies statistical genetics to E. coli and P. aeruginosa pangenomes, linking cis-regulatory variants to expression changes. The items differ in task and organism, and only the first two stress evaluation or analysis design directly. What is new is the move toward context-sensitive, leakage-controlled frameworks and population-scale variant-to-expression mapping in bacteria; the items do not compare these approaches against one another.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
